('23', 'mbe', 'mbe_0023', 'prompt_0071', 'Barbri - Drills and Released Questions - MDR (MBE - 2007)', 'property', '164', '', 'MIXED SUBJECTS single-family residences. O’ Toole notified Sandberg that he, O’Toole, will spend the money to improve the dirt path into a private driveway by paving it and doubling its width to 24 feet. He will also provide appropriate drainage for such an improved driveway by means of ditches on either side of the pavement. Sandberg had ceased using Leftacre as a dairy farm five years earlier, and it has been vacant ever since. Sandberg instituted an appropriate action for a declaratory judgment to establish that O’Toole cannot so improve the dirt path. If Sandberg prevails in such an action, it will be because:', 'Exclude the evidence, because it is not the best evidence.', 'Exclude the evidence, because it is inad- missible hearsay not within any recognized exception.', 'Admit the evidence, because it is a record of a document affecting an interest in property.', 'Admit the evidence, because it constitutes a past recollection recorded. Questions 72-73 are based on the following fact situation: Parker was a guest at Hotel, located about 200 miles from his home. After Parker had spent two nights at Hotel, he received a call at 5 a.m. from his wife, who told him that their child had just MIXED SUBJECTS 165. been rushed to the hospital and was in critical condition. Parker decided to hurry home. He called the airport and reserved space on the next flight out,', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('230', 'mbe', 'mbe_0230', 'prompt_0543', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'criminal', '86', '', 'When Cereal killed Jane, what crime is he most likely to have committed?', 'First-degree murder.', 'Second-degree murder.', 'Voluntary manslaughter.', 'Involuntary manslaughter.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('231', 'mbe', 'mbe_0231', 'prompt_0544', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'criminal', '87', '', 'What is Cereal''s best complete common law defense to the killing of Jane?', 'Insanity.', 'The Rule of Provocation.', 'Mistake of fact.', 'Diminished capacity.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('232', 'mbe', 'mbe_0232', 'prompt_0545', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'criminal', '88', '', 'What is Cereal''s best modern partial defense to the killing of Jane?', 'The Rule of Provocation.', 'Insanity.', 'Imperfect self-defense.', 'Diminished capacity. Questions 89 - 94 are based on the following facts: It was raining quite hard in Los Angeles, but Mason desired to tryout his new Jaguar se- dan, with special anti-lock brakes. Mason was aware that in the rain it was more dan- gerous to drive, but felt, "Why buy an ex- pensive car, if you can''t try it out?" So Mason went driving. In order not to get a chill, Mason had a couple of drinks of fine Perestroyka Brand Vodka (made in New Jer- sey). Mason was a big man, so there was', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('233', 'mbe', 'mbe_0233', 'prompt_0546', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'criminal', '89', '', 'Mason''s not giving aid to the old man was:', 'Battery.', 'Assault.', 'Misprision.', 'No crime.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('234', 'mbe', 'mbe_0234', 'prompt_0547', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'criminal', '90', '', 'Mason''s under-payment to the restau- rant of the food bill was which crime?', 'Larceny.', 'Larceny by trick.', 'Embezzlement.', 'False pretenses.', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('235', 'mbe', 'mbe_0235', 'prompt_0548', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'criminal', '91', '', 'Under the modem law, Mason''s non- payment of the food bill was which, if any, crime:', 'Theft.', 'Petty theft.', 'Grand theft.', 'None ofthe above.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('236', 'mbe', 'mbe_0236', 'prompt_0549', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'criminal', '92', '', 'Teena''s taking of Mason''s car, mod- ernly, was which, if any crime? 1m CRIMINAL LAW', 'Grand theft.', 'Petty theft.', 'Vandalism.', 'No crime, because she had con- structive consent.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('237', 'mbe', 'mbe_0237', 'prompt_0550', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'criminal', '93', '', 'If Teena is convicted of involuntary manslaughter, which theory will, most likely, have been utilized by the pros- ecution?', 'Malum in se unlawful act.', 'Criminal negligence.', 'Sufficiency of the evidence.', 'Depraved heart.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('238', 'mbe', 'mbe_0238', 'prompt_0551', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'criminal', '94', '', 'Is there any theory by which Mason could be charged with the criminal ho- micide of the pedestrian?', 'Yes, he was a substantial factor.', 'Unclean hands.', 'Depraved heart.', 'None of the above.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('239', 'mbe', 'mbe_0239', 'prompt_0552', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'contracts', '95', '', 'Assume that Breach of the Peace is de- fined as follows: The purposeful assembly offour or more individuals in public, with the intent or with reckless disregardfor the tranquillity ofothers present. IfAI, Betty, Chuck and Denise light off firecrackers in Central Park, their best defense to charges of Conspiracy to Breach the Peace and Breach of the Peace is:', 'There was no proof that anyone else was present.', 'This crime has multiple intent requirements - and these have not been proven.', 'Wharton''s Rule.', 'Firecrackers in Central Park are no big deal.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('24', 'mbe', 'mbe_0024', 'prompt_0072', 'Barbri - Drills and Released Questions - MDR (MBE - 2007)', 'property', '73', '', 'Will Parker prevail in his action against the police?', 'Larceny.', 'Attempted larceny.', 'Embezzlement.', 'No crime. Questions 76-77 are based on the following fact situation: Zelda, a paving contractor with an excellent reputation in the community, entered into a written contract with Norman to pave the park- ing lot behind his new store. The contract contained no provision regarding assignment. A few days after they entered into the con- tract, Zelda realized that scheduling difficulties would make it impossible for her to complete the job in the time Norman needed to have the job done. Instead of', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('240', 'mbe', 'mbe_0240', 'prompt_0553', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'contracts', '96', '', 'Finn is an ex-hippie, now yuppie, liv- ing in suburbia. One of Finn''s neigh- bors, Nasty, is very jealous of Finn for his success in trading stocks. One Sat- urday night, Nasty rings Finn''s door- bell and asks whether he can come in. Finn says "Sure." Nasty then offers Finn a marijuana cigarette. Finn exclaims "Whoa, baby - that stuffis illegal- but it was fun and it has been years." Nasty responds, "Keep it my friend." Nasty then calls the police and tells them Finn is in illegal possession of marijuana. The police arrive 10 minutes later, with a fully proper telephonic search war- rant, and find the marijuana in Finn''s home. What is Finn''s best defense to a charge of "Knowing possession of marijuana ?"', 'He lacked knowledge or scienter.', 'He was entrapped, as a matter of law.', 'He was not in true possession.', 'He lacked evil motives, and was a creature of circumstance. CRIMINAL LAW 1m Questions 97 -100 are based on the following facts. Dick was homeless and decided to camp out in the back yard of Marcia. Marcia had quite a big back yard, so at first Dick was not no- ticed. Then one night, Marcia saw Dick and, with gun in hand, threatened to shoot Dick immediately - ifhe did not leave. Dick said, "Madam, I mean no harm; Ijust need a place to sleep." Marcia replied, "Two minutes, or you''re dead." Dick d', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('241', 'mbe', 'mbe_0241', 'prompt_0554', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'contracts', '97', '', 'Under modem majority rules, if Dick is charged with "Criminal Trespass," what is his best defense?', 'Necessity.', 'Duress.', 'Guilt of the Opposing Party.', 'Unclean hands.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('242', 'mbe', 'mbe_0242', 'prompt_0555', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'contracts', '98', '', 'Under the common law, if Dick is charged with Forcible Entry and De- tainer, will he be guilty?', 'Yes, because he trespassed onto Marcia''s land. B CRIMINAL LAW', 'No, because Marcia used exces- sive force.', 'No, because he used no force in the trespass.', 'No, because such was not a com- mon law crime.', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('243', 'mbe', 'mbe_0243', 'prompt_0556', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'contracts', '99', '', 'Under the modern law, if Marcia is charged with aggravated assault, she will most likely:', 'Be found guilty, since deadly force cannot be used to protect property.', 'Be found guilty, since she fired a shot.', 'Be found not guilty, based on the defense of habitation defense.', 'Be found not guilty, because Dick was unlawfully present.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('244', 'mbe', 'mbe_0244', 'prompt_0557', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'contracts', '100', '', 'If Dick is charged with battery of Marcia and robbery of her gun, his best affirmative defense will be:', 'Marcia was the aggressor.', 'Necessity.', 'He did not intend to permanently deprive.', 'Diminished capacity. ENDOFQUESTIONS CRIMINAL LAW ANSWERS A is the "best" answer but it is not the only correct answer. The Columbia stat- ute punishes for substance addiction. But, although the addict likely often has a guilty mental state (the desire to have the drug), he need not necessarily have done any action whatsoever. Crimes which so punish often violate due pro- cess. They are called "status" crimes - because they punish guilty status only. Our system of justice requires that sta- tus n', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('245', 'mbe', 'mbe_0245', 'prompt_0558', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'property', '1', '', 'If Stan sues oil company over the gas, his best theory of recovery will be for:', 'Strict liability for abnormally dangerous activities.', 'Private nuisance.', 'Public nuisance.', 'Allow the action, because Stan is lessee of the land.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('246', 'mbe', 'mbe_0246', 'prompt_0559', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'property', '3', '', 'Oil Company''s best defense to a suit by Stan to enjoin the refinery''s opera- tion is:', 'Stan moved to the nuisance and, thus, cannot complain.', 'The refinery is a social necessity, which is operated in a proper place and in a proper manner.', 'No one else has ever complained about the gas.', 'Stan''s cause of action is against his landlord, not Oil Company.', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('247', 'mbe', 'mbe_0247', 'prompt_0560', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'property', '2', '', 'If Stan sues Oil Company and Oil Com- pany challenges Stan''s standing to sue, the court should:', 'That Stockbroker acted in good faith when he made the state- ment.', 'That Aaron was going to pur- chase the stock anyway.', 'Federal Bank will recover be- cause it is a reasonably foresee- able plaintiff.', 'Federal Bank will recover, but only if it can establish gross neg- ligence on Accountant''s part. In the course of conducting an audit for Brown Company, Accountant carelessly computed asset totals. As a result, the audit report stated that Brown Company was sub- stantially stronger financially than it was. Federal Bank relied on the audit when it approved a $1 million loan to Brown Com- pany. Brown Company is now insolvent and unable to repay the $1 million loan to Fed- eral Bank.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('248', 'mbe', 'mbe_0248', 'prompt_0561', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'contracts', '6', '', 'If Federal Bank decides to sue Accoun- tant, its most promising cause of action is for:', 'Common law deceit.', 'Negligent misrepresentation.', 'Innocent misrepresentation.', 'None of the above.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('249', 'mbe', 'mbe_0249', 'prompt_0562', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'contracts', '7', '', 'In the majority of American jurisdic- tions, if Federal Bank sues Accountant for misrepresentation:', 'Rhonda will have no cause of action for common law deceit, since the element ofa false state- ment of fact has not been met.', 'Rhonda will have no cause of action, since she should have taken the car to a mechanic who could have discovered the defect.', 'Rhonda will have a cause of ac- tion, since active concealment constitutes common law deceit.', 'Rhonda will not have a cause of action for deceit because of the "as is" clause. Questions 10 -11 are based on the following facts: Brakeco is a manufacturer ofhydraulic brake systems. Its chief competitor is Stopco. In an effort to cut into Stopco''s sales, Brakeco distributed a brochure to potential Stopco customers in which Brakeco favorably com- pared its product to Stopco''s. The brochure contained numerous false and derogatory statements of fact about Stopco''s hydraulic brake systems. Six mo', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('25', 'mbe', 'mbe_0025', 'prompt_0073', 'Barbri - Drills and Released Questions - MDR (MBE - 2007)', 'contracts', '76', '', 'With regard to this assignment by Zelda, which of the following statements is true?', 'The assignment is valid only if Norman agrees to accept performance by Kurt.', 'Zelda breached her contract with Norman by assigning it to Kurt with- out his prior consent.', 'Norman must accept performance by Kurt.', 'The assignment is valid, even if Norman objects, as long as Zelda supervises the performance of the contract by Kurt.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('250', 'mbe', 'mbe_0250', 'prompt_0563', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'contracts', '10', '', 'If Stopco decides to sue Brakeco, the most appropriate cause ofaction would be for:', 'Libel.', 'Trade libel.', 'Trade slander.', 'Common law deceit.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('251', 'mbe', 'mbe_0251', 'prompt_0564', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'contracts', '11', '', 'Under common law rules, the likely outcome of Stopco''s suit against Brakeco will be:', 'Stopco will recover because it has sustained a loss in profits.', 'Stopco will recover for general lost profits, plus punitive dam- ages.', 'Stopco will not recover, unless it can show loss of specific cus- tomers as special damages.', 'Stopco will not recover because Brakeco''s activity is an accepted business practice.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('252', 'mbe', 'mbe_0252', 'prompt_0565', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'contracts', '12', '', 'Grey is a major league baseball player. Clay, a manufacturer of sports equip- ment, used Grey''s photograph in a newspaper advertisement for his prod- ucts. While Clay''s products are of high quality, he did not have Grey''s permis- sion to use the picture. In an action by Grey against Clay, Grey will have a cause of action for invasion of privacy under which of the following theories:', 'Public disclosure ofprivate facts.', 'Misappropriation.', 'Intrusion on seclusion.', 'False light. IDiIS II Questions 13 -14 are based on the following facts: Clement had a civil judgment against Lake for $100. Lake refused to pay the award. In an effort to "teach Lake a lesson," Clement had writs ofattachment issued against Lake''s real property, personal property, and bank accounts which had a total value of $500,000. As a result of Clement''s actions, Lake suffered financial loss and consider- able embarrassment.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('253', 'mbe', 'mbe_0253', 'prompt_0566', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'contracts', '13', '', 'Lake is most likely to succeed against Clement under which of the following theories?', 'Malicious civil prosecution.', 'Malicious criminal prosecution.', 'Abuse of process.', 'Conversion.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('254', 'mbe', 'mbe_0254', 'prompt_0567', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'contracts', '14', '', 'In order for Lake to recover against Clement, which ofthe following is Lake not required to establish.', 'The prior proceeding was termi- nated in Lake''s favor.', 'Clement had an ulterior motive for bringing the multiple attach- ments.', 'Lake suffered damages as a re- sult of Clement''s actions. f21lGtlS', 'Lake''s excessive attachments was intentional. Questions 15 - 16 are based on the following facts: Laura, a famous diplomat, entered into a contract with X College, in which she agreed to teach international relations for one year. Because of her special expertise, the con- tract provided a large salary. Under the contract''s terms, Laura agreed she would not teach at another college in the same city during the contract term. Y College, which is located in the same city as X College, learned about', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('255', 'mbe', 'mbe_0255', 'prompt_0568', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'contracts', '16', '', 'If X College brings an action against Laura based on the contract, the most likely result will be:', 'Laura will be forced to work for X College.', 'Laura can work for Y College.', 'The negative covenant will be found unenforceable because it is unreasonable.', 'The court will enforce the nega- tive covenant. TOOlS E Questions 17 - 18 are based on the following facts: Logan and Adam were students in Profes- sor Smith''s Physics class. Much to Profes- sor Smith''s disgust, the students were con- versing loudly during his lecture. Professor Smith decided to "get Logan''s and Adam''s attention" by throwing a pen over their heads. The students saw the pen coming to- ward them and ducked to avoid being hit by it. Unfortunately, the pen struck Davey, a student se', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('256', 'mbe', 'mbe_0256', 'prompt_0569', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'contracts', '17', '', 'If Logan and Adam sue Professor Smith, the most likely outcome will be:', 'Logan andAdam will recover for intentional infliction of emo- tional distress.', 'Logan andAdam will recover for assault.', 'Logan and Adam will not re- cover because the pen did not strike them.', 'Logan and Adam will not re- cover because Professor Smith did not intend to hit them with the pen.', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('257', 'mbe', 'mbe_0257', 'prompt_0570', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'contracts', '18', '', 'If Davey sues Professor Smith for the injury to his eye:', 'Calvin will not recover because he was a trespasser and the land was properly posted.', 'Calvin will not recover because he exceeded the scope of his li- cense.', 'Calvin will not recover because Eunice was justified in protect- ing her property.', 'Calvin will recover. Eunice owns a tract of undeveloped land in Madison County. Every autumn, she has had a problem with hunters who enter her land without permission to shoot geese. In an ef- fort to discourage hunters, she posted large "no trespassing" signs in conspicuous places along the property''s boundary. She also dug a number of deep holes, which she covered with brush to catch any intruders. Calvin, a local land surveyor, was retained to survey the parcel of land adjoining Eunice''s. Whi', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('258', 'mbe', 'mbe_0258', 'prompt_0571', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'property', '19', '', 'In an action by Eunice against Calvin for trespass to land, the likely outcome will be:', 'Eunice will recover because Calvin is a licensee who has ex- ceeded the scope of his license.', 'Eunice will recover, even if the land was not damaged.', 'Eunice will not recover because Calvin did not intend to trespass.', 'Eunice will not recover, unless she can prove some actual dam- age.', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('259', 'mbe', 'mbe_0259', 'prompt_0572', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'property', '21', '', 'In a negligence suit against Sherry, if Alfred raises the claim of negligence per se, the court should:', 'Apply the doctrine because the thing speaks for itself.', 'Apply the doctrine because Alfred was within the class of persons the statute was designed to protect.', 'Reject the doctrine because the reasonably prudent person test is the universal standard of care in negligence cases.', 'Reject the doctrine because Pe- ter was contributorily negligent.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('26', 'mbe', 'mbe_0026', 'prompt_0074', 'Barbri - Drills and Released Questions - MDR (MBE - 2007)', 'contracts', '77', '', 'Assume for the purposes of this question only that Kurt paved the parking lot under the contract, but Norman did not realize that Kurt was substituting for Zelda until the job was half complete. Upon learning that, Norman let Kurt complete the job. If Kurt fails to perform the job in accordance with the original terms of the agreement between Zelda and Norman, Norman:', 'Has a cause of action only against Kurt for damages.', 'Has a cause of action against Kurt and Zelda for damages.', 'Has no cause of action against Kurt, because he and Kurt are not in privity of contract.', 'Has no cause of action against Zelda, because he accepted performance from Kurt. Question 78 Developer owned a large tract of land that she had surveyed by a licensed surveyor and then MIXED SUBJECTS 167. subdivided into numerous lots. At the time of the survey, the surveyor drove wooden stakes into the ground to mark the boundaries. The surveyor then made a plat of the survey and recorded the plat in the County Recorder of Deeds Office. On June 1, Andrew purchased Lot 20 in the tract from Devel', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('260', 'mbe', 'mbe_0260', 'prompt_0573', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'property', '22', '', 'Sherry''s best defense to a negligence action by Alfred is: II TQiIS', 'Alfred had the last clear chance to avoid being hit by the car.', 'Peter''s actions were a supersed- ing cause of Alfred''s injuries.', 'The acts of an intentional tort- feasor always break the chain of causation.', 'Sherry''s liability insurance policy does not cover injuries committed by a negligent car thief.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('261', 'mbe', 'mbe_0261', 'prompt_0574', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'property', '23', '', 'IfAlfred sues Peter for his injuries, the likely result will be:', 'Peter will be liable because the injuries were suffered while Pe- ter was stealing the car.', 'Peter will be liable, if he failed to exercise the care expected of a reasonable, prudent twelve- year-old when he drove the car.', 'Peter will be liable, if he failed to exercise the care expected of a reasonable, prudent person when he drove the car.', 'Peter will not be liable because a twelve-year-old does not have the capacity to be negligent.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('262', 'mbe', 'mbe_0262', 'prompt_0575', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'evidence', '25', '', 'Glenda, a bicyclist, was injured when John lost control of his automobile while rounding a curve on a local high- way. In a single lawsuit, Glenda sued John for negligently operating his au- tomobile and also sued State Highway Department for negligent design of the curve. In a special verdict, the jury found John to be 75% responsible for Glenda''s injuries and State Highway Department 25% responsible. State Highway Department is not immune from a damages judgment. In a state that follows cornmon law prin- ciples, if John is insolvent:', 'Glenda can recover the entire judgment from State Highway Department.', 'Glenda can recover only 25% of the judgment from State High- way Department because of the rule of indemnification.', 'Glenda can recover only 25% of the judgment from State High- way Department because of the rule of contribution.', 'Glenda cannot recover anything from State Highway Department on the judgment. milS II Questions 26 - 27 are based on the following facts: Miller recently purchased a lOG-acre farm in the State of Columbia. Power Company has an easement across Miller''s property, which includes the authority to trim trees that might interfere with Power Company''s elec- trical lines. Denton, an employee of Power Company, entered Miller''s property to check the electrical lines. Denton fell into an aban- doned well a', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('263', 'mbe', 'mbe_0263', 'prompt_0576', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'property', '26', '', 'While on Miller''s land, the law would characterize Denton as a:', 'Trespasser.', 'Business invitee.', 'Social guest.', 'Licensee.', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('264', 'mbe', 'mbe_0264', 'prompt_0577', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'property', '27', '', 'If Denton sues Miller for negligence, a court applying common law rules will find:', 'Miller owes no duty of care to Denton.', 'Miller''s duty of care is limited to warning Denton of dangers known by Miller. f2i1 TffilS', 'Miller''s duty of care is to warn Denton of dangers known by Miller and to inspect his prop- erty for unknown dangers.', 'Miller''s duty ofcare is that ofthe reasonable, prudent landowner in the circumstances. Questions 28 - 29 are based on the following facts: Arlen was driving his van on Route 12 when he noticed he was running out of gas. He stopped and parked the van on the shoulder of the highway. The van''s rear bumper ex- tended one-halffoot into the highway. Betty, who was driving an automobile in the same direction on Route 12, approached Arlen''s van from the rear. Betty was traveling at an excessive rate of', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('265', 'mbe', 'mbe_0265', 'prompt_0578', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'property', '28', '', 'In a jurisdiction that has not adopted comparative fault and still follows com- mon law principles, Arlen will:', 'Recover $100,000 from Betty.', 'Recover $95,000 from Betty.', 'Not recover anything from Betty because Arlen was contributorily negligent.', 'Not recover anything from Betty because Arlen assumed the risk.', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('266', 'mbe', 'mbe_0266', 'prompt_0579', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'property', '30', '', 'The State of Columbia has enacted a comparative negligence statute which provides: "The negligence of the in- jured party will not bar recovery against another person, so long as the injured party''s negligence was not greater than the negligence of the person against whom recovery is sought. Any damages shall be diminished in proportion to the amount ofnegligence attributable to the injured party." Tom and Wanda were involved in an automobile accident, in which Wanda sustained personal injury damages totaling $100,000. The jury returned a special verdict wherein it found Tom 40% and Wanda 60% at fault, respectively, for the accident. Under Columbia''s comparative negli- gence statute, Wanda will recover from Tom:', '$100,000. TOOlS Ell', '$60,000.', '$40,000.', 'Nothing. IS DtiS Questions 31 - 35 are based on the following fact situation: Gasco stores and manufactures acid gas on its premises. Early one morning a leak de- veloped in the connecting lines between the tanks. The leak was not the fault of Gasco. The escaping gas forms a greenish cloud. Before the greenish cloud ofacid gas reached the point of dispersal it made contact with a cable repairman who was repairing a cable atop a telephone pole. The repairman did not see the cloud and he inhaled t', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('267', 'mbe', 'mbe_0267', 'prompt_0580', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'property', '31', '', 'In a suit by cable repairman against Gasco, which of the following legal theories should he base his cause of action?', 'Battery', 'Private nuisance.', 'Strict liability.', 'Negligence.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('268', 'mbe', 'mbe_0268', 'prompt_0581', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'property', '32', '', 'Gasco took every possible precaution to prevent this type of accident. Will Gasco be free from liability to repair- man?', 'Yes, because the exercise of ex- treme care was used.', 'Yes, because there is no fault.', 'No, because the gas was negli- gently stored.', 'No, because this was an activity involving a serious degree of danger.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('269', 'mbe', 'mbe_0269', 'prompt_0582', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'torts', '33', '', 'Gasco asserts the defense of contribu- tory negligence for failure ofrepainnan to see the greenish cloud. Gasco will:', 'Win, because repairman as- sumed the risk.', 'Win, because repairman was negligent.', 'Lose, because contributory neg- ligence is not a defense to strict liability.', 'Lose, because repainnan was not negligent.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('27', 'mbe', 'mbe_0027', 'prompt_0082', 'Barbri - Drills and Released Questions - MDR (MBE - 2007)', 'evidence', '176', '', 'MIXED SUBJECTS Question 106 State B’s legislature passed a statute that required every used car sold in the state to be tested prior to sale to determine whether it was in compliance with a set of strict exhaust emis- sion standards that were also included in the legislation. Used cars would have to be brought up to standard and pass the emissions test prior to sale. Certain persons in the state object to the legislation because one of its results will be to raise the average price of used cars in State B. Only cars to be sold for junk are exempt from the statute. Among the following, who would be most likely to have standing to raise a constitutional challenge to the legislation?', 'Growit commenced the manufacture of Bugoff at the South Freedonia plant three years before Clampett acquired the land adjacent to the plant.', 'Federal, state, and local agencies approved the design of the plant and equipment used to produce Bugoff.', 'The principal users of Bugoff are State and Federal Departments of Agriculture. (D i Bugoff is the only pesticide that can safely and effectively kill the Potato Maggot, which, if not controlled, would destroy North Freedonia’s potato crop, its principal product. Questions 108-109 are based on the following fact situation: While Doug was driving Olivia’s car, he hit Peter in a pedestrian right-of-way. Peter sued both Doug and Olivia, alleging that Doug had negligently driven the car and that Oli', 'An out-of-state manufacturer who might be required to indemnify its dealers in State B for costs arising from the statute. Question 107 The state of North Freedonia has long had a reputation for growing the biggest and tastiest potatoes in the country. Growers of potatoes in North Freedonia recently began spraying Bugoff onto their crops to prevent the spread of the Potato Maggot, an insect that can destroy young potato plants. Bugoff is manufactured exclusively by Growit, Inc., at its plant in', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('270', 'mbe', 'mbe_0270', 'prompt_0583', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'torts', '34', '', 'Assume, for this question, that some of the gas settled on an antique car in a Beverly Hills antique car lot. The car was damaged beyond repair. Will the car lot have an action against Gasco?', 'No, if this type of damage is un- foreseeable from the activity conducted by Gasco.', 'No, because only the original market value of the cars are pro- tected.', 'Yes, because the car lot was only one block from the Gasco manu- facturing plant.', 'No, because strict liability does not compensate for loss to prop- erty.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('271', 'mbe', 'mbe_0271', 'prompt_0584', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'torts', '35', '', 'Assume that Neighbor sees the green- ish cloud approaching repainnan, and, knowing that it is acid gas, runs out to assist repainnan who had fallen to the ground. As Neighbor is running to re- painnan, she trips in a gopher hole and breaks her leg. Will Neighbor prevail in an action against Gasco?', 'No, because neighbor assumed the risk.', 'No, because Neighbor''s conduct was unforeseeable.', 'Yes, because Gasco was engag- ing in abnonnally dangerous ac- tivities.', 'Yes, since Gasco put repainnan in peril, they are responsible to any foreseeable rescuer.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('272', 'mbe', 'mbe_0272', 'prompt_0585', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'torts', '36', '', 'Mrs. Shopper went into Happytime Yogurt Shoppe to get change for her parking meter. As Mrs. Shopper entered the shop, the door closer snapped and struck Mrs. Shopper on the head and rendered her unconscious. In a cause of action for negligence, Mrs. Shopper''s best theory would be? TffilS B', 'Res Ipsa Loquitur.', 'She was owed a duty ofcare as a business invitee.', 'She was owed a duty ofcare as a licensee.', 'None of the above. EDI TCJ1IS Questions 37 - 38 are based on the following facts: Pete was a passenger in a car driven by Kent. Kent suddenly lost control of the car and swerved off the shoulder of the road. The car then overturned and rolled down the embankment. Pete was killed on impact. Kent stated that the loose rocks and gravel on the road might have caused him to lose control of the car, but he could not be defi- nite.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('273', 'mbe', 'mbe_0273', 'prompt_0586', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'torts', '37', '', 'The best theory of recovery for Pete is?', 'Res Ipsa Loquitur.', 'Wrongful death.', 'Survival statute.', 'Substantial factor.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('274', 'mbe', 'mbe_0274', 'prompt_0587', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'torts', '38', '', 'If the court finds Res Ipsa Loquitur, the result would be?', 'Binding on the jury.', 'Kent would win.', 'Pete would win.', 'An inference of negligence is created. Questions 39 - 41 are based on the following facts: Otis is the owner of a small paint and hard- ware store. One of Otis'' clerks had negli- gently stacked a number of rolled window shades behind the customer service counter. The area behind the customer service counter was not open to the public. Mrs. Patron en- tered the store one afternoon looking for some vinyl tile, accompanied by Martha, her 6-year-old daughter. Martha walked unseen behind the counter', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('275', 'mbe', 'mbe_0275', 'prompt_0588', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'torts', '39', '', 'In order for Martha to recover for her injuries, an appropriate action would be:', 'Negligence.', 'Doctrine of Respondeat Supe- nor.', 'Strict Liability.', 'There is no appropriate action because Otis was not negligent.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('276', 'mbe', 'mbe_0276', 'prompt_0589', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'torts', '40', '', 'Martha''s best theory in holding Otis li- able for her injuries is:', 'She was a business invitee.', 'She was owed a duty as a child trespasser.', 'Attractive Nuisance Doctrine.', 'Inspection would have revealed a patent defect.', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('277', 'mbe', 'mbe_0277', 'prompt_0590', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'torts', '41', '', 'If Otis asserts the defense of contribu- tory negligence, he will:', 'Win, because Martha assumed the risk.', 'Win, because Martha caused her own injuries.', 'Lose, because the attractive nui- sance doctrine negates contribu- tory negligence.', 'Lose, because store owners are strictly liable for foreseeable in- Junes.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('278', 'mbe', 'mbe_0278', 'prompt_0591', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'torts', '42', '', 'A and B are hunting in the forest. Each, at the same time, fires his shotgun. A pellet from one of the shotguns strikes C in the leg. What is C''s best theory of recovery?', 'Substantial factor.', 'Alternative liability.', '"But For."', 'None of the above. IDiIS EDJ', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('279', 'mbe', 'mbe_0279', 'prompt_0592', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'torts', '45', '', 'Two teenage girls went into the ABC classmates. One morning, as her class- department store. The security guard, mate Mary was about to sit in her chair, who was always suspicious of teenage Sue pulled the chair from under her. shoppers, asked the girls to remove their Surprised by Sue''s conduct, Mary fell coats and to empty their purses. After down and was humiliated by the inci- about 10 minutes, the guard, convinced dent. Mary can most likely make a that the girls had not taken anything, claim against Sue for: apologized and let the girls leave. If the girls bring a cause of action for false', 'Win, since the store had a privi-', 'Win, because the girls were, at all times, free to leave.', 'Assault and battery. lege to detain them.', 'Infliction of emotional distress.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('28', 'mbe', 'mbe_0028', 'prompt_0086', 'Barbri - Drills and Released Questions - MDR (MBE - 2007)', 'contracts', '178', '', 'MIXED SUBJECTS according to the plans of Architect. Without the additional lot repair work, the residence cannot be constructed at all. Must Builder perform the contract?', 'No, if the increased costs of construc- tion would bankrupt him.', 'No, the contract is void because of mutual mistake.', 'No, Builder is discharged of his obligation because of impracticability of performance.', 'Yes, but he may bring an action against Owner for the increased costs of construction.', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('280', 'mbe', 'mbe_0280', 'prompt_0593', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'torts', '46', '', 'Janice saw a demonstration of a hair dryer made by Hairco, that dried hair', 'Prevail, because the dryer was', 'Not liable, because jogger''s con- utes to dry her hair and that the dryer duct was acceptable under the made a loud noise. Janice called Hairco circumstances. who assured her that some dryers were just louder than others. Several days', 'Liable for battery. later, while using the dryer, Janice re- ceived a severe shock which caused her', 'Liable for assault. physical injury. In an action against Hairco, Janice will most likely: 1m 1CJ''11S', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('281', 'mbe', 'mbe_0281', 'prompt_0594', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'torts', '47', '', 'Al was hit by an automobile negligently driven by Robert. Al was immediately', 'Strict liability. taken to the hospital for treatment. At the hospital, he developed severe un-', 'Battery. controlled internal bleeding, as a result of pre-existing hemophilia. He died the', 'Negligence. next day from the bleeding. What ef- fect will AI''s pre-existing hemophilia', 'Breach of warranty. have on recovery of his estate''s recov- ery of damages?', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('282', 'mbe', 'mbe_0282', 'prompt_0595', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'torts', '49', '', 'Sam, a large 6''2" man, was at a sports bar watching the USC Trojans pummel', 'Win, since he felt apprehension damages from Robert. when George was shaking his fist at him.', 'Win, since George was shaking his fist.', 'Lose, since in light of George''s statement, his conduct was not sufficient to create immediate ap- prehension in Sam.', 'Lose, because George is not big enough to assault Sam.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('283', 'mbe', 'mbe_0283', 'prompt_0596', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'torts', '50', '', 'Bob and Mary lived in a remote area of town. One evening, Bob heard a noise outside. He grabbed his gun and pro- ceeded to look around his front yard. Thinking he saw a wolf, he fired a shot and killed the animal. It turned out that the animal was the neighbor''s dog Spot. Which of the following propositions is most correct?', 'Neighbor will have a cause of action for trespass to chattel.', 'Bob will not be liable to neigh- bor because he made a reason- able mistake.', 'Neighbor will have a cause of action for battery.', 'Bob will be found guilty of cru- elty to animals. Blnlls Questions 51 - 54 are based on the following facts: Marvin was dissatisfied with his facial fea- tures, especially the large "bags" under his eyes. He finally decided to consider elec- tive plastic surgery. Marvin visited the of- fice of Doctor, a certified licensed plastic surgeon. Doctor advised Marvin that if he surgically removed the bags, Marvin would "look years younger." Marvin agreed to un- dergo the surgery, at Doctor''s office as', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('284', 'mbe', 'mbe_0284', 'prompt_0597', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'torts', '51', '', 'IfMarvin sues Doctor for removing the mole:', 'Marvin will not recover, so long as Doctor performed the surgery as would a reasonable, prudent plastic surgeon.', 'Marvin will not recover because he impliedly consented to Doctor''s removal of the mole.', 'Marvin will recover because Doctor has committed the tort of battery.', 'Marvin will recover because Doctor was negligent.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('285', 'mbe', 'mbe_0285', 'prompt_0598', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'torts', '52', '', 'If Marvin contends that Doctor was re- sponsible for Marvin''s loss ofsensation in his face, the likely outcome will be:', 'Marvin will recover, if he can show that Doctor had exclusive control of his person during the surgery and that the injury suf- fered usually would not occur in the absence of negligence.', 'Marvin will recover because a surgeon is always responsible for a patient''s injuries during sur- gery.', 'Marvin will not recover, unless a percipient witness testifies that Doctor''s negligence was the cause in fact of the injury.', 'None of the above.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('286', 'mbe', 'mbe_0286', 'prompt_0599', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'torts', '53', '', 'The standard of care that the court should apply in Marvin''s negligence action against Doctor is that of:', 'The reasonable, prudent physi- cian.', 'The reasonable, prudent plastic surgeon in Doctor''s locality.', 'The reasonable, prudent plastic surgeon as determined by na- tional standards.', 'The reasonable, prudent plastic surgeon with education and ex- perience similar to Doctor''s.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('287', 'mbe', 'mbe_0287', 'prompt_0600', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'torts', '54', '', 'Assume, for purposes of this question only, that Marvin''s loss of sensation in his face resulted from the operation around his eyes, but that Doctor was not negligent in performing the surgery. In a suit by Marvin against Doctor:', 'Marvin will recover because a surgeon is responsible for all in- juries a patient sustains during an operation.', 'Marvin will recover under a neg- ligence theory, if Doctor failed to inform him that loss of facial sensation is a common side ef- fect of surgery around the eyes.', 'Marvin will recover because of the rule of post hoc ergo propter hoc.', 'Marvin will not recover. TOOlS 1m Questions 55 - 57 are based on the following facts: Nancy owns a collection agency. She prides herself on collecting debts where other col- lection agencies have failed. Nancy at- tempted to collect a debt that Darren owed one of her clients. She made numerous tele- phone calls to Darren''s home between the hours of 11:00 P.M. and 6:00 AM., to in- duce him to pay the debt. She continued this practice for over two months. Finally, Nancy visited Darren''s home and t', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('288', 'mbe', 'mbe_0288', 'prompt_0601', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'torts', '61', '', 'IfIrene sues Hank and Trucker in neg- ligence to recover damages for her in- juries:', 'Irene will recover only those damages which she can prove each defendant caused.', 'Hank and Trucker will each be liable to Irene for the full amount of her damages.', 'Hank and Trucker will each be liable for fifty percent of Irene''s damages.', 'Irene will recover nothing be- cause she assumed the risk.', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('289', 'mbe', 'mbe_0289', 'prompt_0602', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'torts', '62', '', 'Assume, for this question, that a jury determined that Hank and Trucker were both responsible for Irene''s damages and that Hank paid Irene the full amount. Hank''s best recourse against Trucker would be under the theory of:', 'Satisfaction.', 'Indemnification.', 'Release.', 'Contribution.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('29', 'mbe', 'mbe_0029', 'prompt_0094', 'Barbri - Drills and Released Questions - MDR (MBE - 2007)', 'torts', '182', '', 'MIXED SUBJECTS John get out of the way, John said, “Try to make me, scab!” Carter, intending to frighten John, swung his hammer at him. The head on the hammer, however, was defective and it flew off, hitting John in the face. John sues Carter for battery. Most likely he will:', 'There was no malice on the part of the defendant.', 'The newspaper’s retraction negated any harm.', 'Snoops got his information from a secre- tary at the Medical School.', 'Wally is dead. Questions 127-128 are based on the following fact situation: Christine and her friend Zelda were going away to college and had quite a few personal belongings to transport. Christine’s father offered to drive them in his van, but when the van was loaded they discovered there was only room for two people, so Christine asked her boyfriend Harry to drive her in his car while Christine’s father and Zelda rode in the van. About halfway to the college, while the van and Harry’s car were', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('290', 'mbe', 'mbe_0290', 'prompt_0603', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'property', '63', '', 'Assume, for this question, that Hank''s negligence was the sole cause of the accident and that the jurisdiction has an automobile guest statute. If Irene sues Hank for damages:', 'Irene will recover because Hank was negligent.', 'Irene will recover only if she can show Hank''s conduct amounted to gross negligence.', 'Irene will recover only if she can show Hank''s conduct amounted to an intentional act.', 'Irene will recover nothing. Questions 64 - 65 are based on the following facts: Paul was a member of a professional base- ball team that was involved in a heated com- petition for the league championship. His team was playing its arch rival in an impor- tant late-season game. With his team down by one run with one out in the final inning, Paul was on first base. When a ground ball was hit to the second baseman, Paul decided he would "break up" a potential double play by making a hard, but legal,', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('291', 'mbe', 'mbe_0291', 'prompt_0604', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'property', '64', '', 'If Tom sues Paul for the injury to his ankle, his most likely cause of action will be for:', 'Assault.', 'Battery.', 'Intentional infliction of emo- tional distress.', 'Strict liability.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('292', 'mbe', 'mbe_0292', 'prompt_0605', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'property', '65', '', 'Paul''s best defense to Tom''s suit for injury to his ankle would be:', 'Contributory negligence.', 'Justification.', 'Implied consent.', 'Private necessity. lGllS EJ Questions 66 - 69 are based on the following facts: Scott owns a furniture store in the town of Smallville. Scott advertises his location by means of a large neon sign attached to the building. The sign, which extends out over a public sidewalk approximately 15 feet from the ground, is anchored by two large cables. Scott had not checked the condition of the cables in years, so he was unaware that they were severely corroded. During an unusu- ally violent windstorm, th', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('293', 'mbe', 'mbe_0293', 'prompt_0606', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'property', '66', '', 'At common law, most courts would classify Pam as:', 'A trespasser.', 'A licensee.', 'An invitee.', 'None of the above.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('294', 'mbe', 'mbe_0294', 'prompt_0607', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'property', '67', '', 'If Pam sues Scott in negligence for her injuries, a court will likely find that Scott:', 'Had a duty of care to keep the cables in good repair.', 'Had a duty of care to warn Pam only of known, latent defects.', 'Had a duty of care to warn Pam only of dangerous active opera- tions on his property.', 'Had no duty of care to Pam.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('295', 'mbe', 'mbe_0295', 'prompt_0608', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'property', '68', '', 'The effect of the unusually violent windstorm on Pam''s negligence suit will likely be to:', 'Prevent Pam from recovering from Scott, because the storm was an intervening act of God.', 'Prevent Pam from recovering from Scott, because a violent storm is not a reasonably fore- seeable event.', 'Not adversely affect Pam''s suit, as a defendant is liable for all injuries directly caused by him.', 'Not adversely affect Pam''s suit, because the storm was a reason- ably foreseeable intervening act of God.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('296', 'mbe', 'mbe_0296', 'prompt_0609', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'property', '69', '', 'Assume, for this question, that Scott''s negligence caused the sign to strike Pam. Pam will be able to recover:', 'Only those damages which Scott could have reasonably foreseen under the circumstances. CI', 'Only those damages which an ordinary person would have suf- fered, because Scott is not re- sponsible for Pam''s preexisting condition.', 'All damages sustained in the ac- cident, even though Pam has a rare bone disease that makes her more susceptible to injury.', 'None of the above. Questions 70 — 74 are based on the following facts: Grover, a civilian employee of the United States Army, was responsible for delivering packages between military bases. He drove a small government-owned van when mak- ing his deliveries. One day, Grover was driv- ing on a public highway and, because he was careless, he lost control of the van. The ve- hicle jumped the curb and struck Inge, a pe- destrian. Inge was seriously injured in the accident. She lingered in the hospita', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('297', 'mbe', 'mbe_0297', 'prompt_0610', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'property', '70', '', 'At common law, if Inge''s estate sued Grover to recover for medical expenses incurred between the date of the acci- dent and the date of her death, the re- sult would be:', 'Inge''s estate would recover medical expenses in a wrongful death action.', 'Inge''s estate would recover medical expenses in a survival action.', 'Inge''s estate would recover noth- ing because her right of action against Grover ended with her death.', 'None of the above. TaTTS 245', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('298', 'mbe', 'mbe_0298', 'prompt_0611', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'torts', '71', '', 'In most states today, ifInge''s husband and minor children wish to recover for lost financial support, services, and so- ciety suffered as a result of her death, their claim against Grover will be:', 'A wrongful death action.', 'A survival action.', 'A negligent infliction of emo- tional distress action.', 'A simple negligence action.', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('299', 'mbe', 'mbe_0299', 'prompt_0612', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'torts', '72', '', 'Assume, for this question, that Inge''s husband and children have a valid neg- ligence claim against Grover. If they wish to hold the Army responsible for Grover''s negligence, they should raise the doctrine of:', 'Negligent hiring.', 'Respondeat superior.', 'Nondelegable duty.', 'Independent contractor.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('3', 'mbe', 'mbe_0003', 'prompt_0029', 'Barbri - Drills and Released Questions - MDR (MBE - 2007)', 'evidence', '52', '', 'EVIDENCE Question 10 Debra’s car struck Peter, a pedestrian, and sped away from the scene of the accident. The car was linked to Debra, but she denies that she was driving the car when Peter was hit and injured. Peter sued Debra for his injuries. The only eyewitness, other than Peter, was Willie, a six-year-old child. During the trial, Peter put Willie on the stand, and Willie testified that he saw Debra driving the car that hit Peter. The defense elected not to cross-examine Willie. The plaintiff’s next witness was Schrinkov, a child psychologist. Schrinkov, who had never met Willie before, was prepared to testify that, based on his observations of Willie on the witness stand, it was highly probable that Willie was telling the truth. Schrinkov was qualified as an expert witness with many years of experience dealing with children. However, the defense objected to any questions regarding the veracity of Willie. Should Schrinkov be allowed to testify about Willie?', 'No, unless there has been no other evi- dence about overheating of the tanks presented by Julio.', 'No, because Julio’s testimony is inadmis- sible hearsay.', 'Yes, because Julio’s testimony is evidence of a fact in issue.', 'Yes, because Julio’s testimony is evidence of a verbal act. GO ON TO THE NEXT PAGE', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('30', 'mbe', 'mbe_0030', 'prompt_0102', 'Barbri - Drills and Released Questions - MDR (MBE - 2007)', 'criminal', '186', '', 'MIXED SUBJECTS Question 136 Vincent, who operated a local neighborhood liquor store, was robbed by a man wielding an unusual knife with a pearl-studded handle. Davis was arrested and charged with armed robbery of Vincent. At trial the prosecution calls Wilma to testify that, three days after the rob- bery of Vincent, she was robbed by Davis with a knife that had a pearl-studded handle. The court will most likely hold that Wilma’s testimony is:', 'West and Brown, as joint tenants with right of survivorship.', 'West and Brown, as tenants in common.', 'Hines and West, as tenants by the entirety.', 'Hines and West, as joint tenants with right of survivorship. Question 140 Sonny was arrested for shoplifting. As Sonny was being booked at the police station, Sonny’s mother, Betty, arrived at the station. As she was talking with Sonny, Photog, a reporter for the Daily News, took Betty’s picture. The photo- graph of Betty appeared on the front page of the next day’s edition of the Daily News. The story of Sonny’s arrest appeared just below it. A caption to the photograph identified Betty as Sonn', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('300', 'mbe', 'mbe_0300', 'prompt_0613', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'torts', '73', '', 'Assume, for this question, that, at the time of the accident, Grover was on a personal errand totally unrelated to Army business. If Inge''s husband and children sue theArmy for Grover''s neg- ligence, the Army''s best defense will be:', 'Grover was acting within the scope of his employment. 1m mils', 'Grover''s actions were a super- seding cause.', 'Grover was engaged in a frolic and detour.', 'The Army''s duty was non- delegable.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('301', 'mbe', 'mbe_0301', 'prompt_0614', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'torts', '74', '', 'Assume, for this question, that Grover was negligent and that the Army was vicariously liable. Ifthe Army raises the defense of sovereign immunity, the re- sult will be:', 'The Army will be liable because sovereign immunity violates the Equal Protection Clause of the Fourteenth Amendment.', 'The Army will be liable because Congress waived sovereign im- munity in negligence cases through the Federal Tort Claims Act.', 'The Army will be liable because agencies of the United States government have never enjoyed sovereign immunity.', 'The Army will not be liable. Questions 75 - 77 are based on the following facts: The Smalltown Sentinel is a local weekly newspaper. Each issue includes a column detailing births, deaths, marriages and other events of interest in the community. In a re- cent issue, the Sentinel ran an item stating: "Local resident Sally Smith, age 21, gave birth to twins last week." There is one Smalltown resident named Sally Smith and she is 21, but she is not married and has never had a child. Smith suffered r', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('302', 'mbe', 'mbe_0302', 'prompt_0615', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'torts', '75', '', 'At common law, ifSally Smith sued the Sentinel for libel based on the news item:', 'She would recover because the statement is libel per se.', 'She would not recover because there has been no publication.', 'She would not recover because she has not established the col- loquium.', 'She would recover, so long as she produced sufficient evidence to show reasonable innuendo.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('303', 'mbe', 'mbe_0303', 'prompt_0616', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'torts', '76', '', 'If the Sentinel can prove that the item was published as a result of an inno- cent mistake, at common law the effect on Sally Smith''s libel suit would be:', 'Her suit would fail because libel required malicious intent at com- mon law.', 'Her suit would fail because libel required at least reckless conduct at common law.', 'Her suit would fail because libel required at least negligence at common law.', 'Her suit would succeed.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('304', 'mbe', 'mbe_0304', 'prompt_0617', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'torts', '77', '', 'Assume, for this question, that Sally Smith has proven a prima facie case for libel. The effect of the Sentinel''s retrac- tion and apology would be:', 'To limit damages the Sentinel must pay, because of the mitiga- tion rule.', 'To limit damages the Sentinel must pay, because of the collat- eral source rule.', 'To limit damages the Sentinel must pay, because of the offset- ting benefit rule.', 'To prevent Sally Smith from re- covering any damages from the Sentinel. TCJilS EID Questions 78 - 81 are based on the following facts: Roman is the host ofa nationally-syndicated radio show. Roman is very controversial and specializes in exposing unsavory details about the lives of politicians and celebrities. Roman decided to exploit his notoriety by marketing a series ofcassette tapes entitled, "Lifestyles of the Sick and Sleazy." The in- formation on the tapes had never been re- vealed or pub', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('305', 'mbe', 'mbe_0305', 'prompt_0618', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'property', '78', '', 'Modernly, if Linda sues Roman for defamation, the most appropriate cause of action would be:', 'Slander.', 'Libel.', 'Slander per se.', 'Libel per quod. 1m TOilS', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('306', 'mbe', 'mbe_0306', 'prompt_0619', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'property', '79', '', 'If Linda brings a defamation action against Cleo, the result will be:', 'Cleo will be liable for republish- ing the defamatory statement.', 'Cleo will be vicariously liable.', 'Cleo will not be liable because he is a secondary publisher.', 'Cleo will be liable, but he may have indemnification from Ro- man.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('307', 'mbe', 'mbe_0307', 'prompt_0620', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'property', '80', '', 'If Linda sues Roman for libel, the rul- ings ofthe United States Supreme Court require that she must prove:', 'Preponderance of the evidence.', 'Clear and convincing evidence.', 'Beyond a reasonable doubt.', 'None of the above. Questions 82 — 85 are based on the following facts: Harriet and Jenny were once general part- ners in the same law firm. Harriet and Jenny had numerous disagreements which led them to dissolve the firm. After the breakup, Harriet learned that Jenny was telling other lawyers that Harriet was facing disciplinary action by the state bar for commingling cli- ent trust fund monies with her personal as- sets. This allegation was untrue in all re- spects. She also learned that Jenny', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('308', 'mbe', 'mbe_0308', 'prompt_0621', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'property', '82', '', 'At common law, if Harriet sues Jenny for defamation with respect to the state- ments about commingling client trust funds, the appropriate cause of action would be:', 'Slander per se.', 'Slander per quod.', 'Simple slander.', 'Intentional interference with pro- spective business advantage.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('309', 'mbe', 'mbe_0309', 'prompt_0622', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'property', '83', '', 'Modernly, if Harriet sues Jenny for defamation with respect to the state- ment about her being a clown, the likely result would be: TORTS 249', 'Harriet will not recover because the statement is Jenny''s opinion and all opinions are constitution- ally protected under the Gertz v. Welch decision.', 'Harriet will not recover because lawyers are always public figures under New York Times v. Sullivan.', 'Harriet will not recover because Jenny''s statement is an opinion that does not appear to be founded on underlying false facts.', 'Harriet will recover.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('31', 'mbe', 'mbe_0031', 'prompt_0105', 'Barbri - Drills and Released Questions - MDR (MBE - 2007)', 'property', '147', '', 'On December 1, Diane died without chil- dren and without a will. The applicable law of intestate succession provides that George is Diane’s only heir. Sam claims that George has no interest in the land. Title to the avocado ranch is held by whom?', 'George, because of the doctrine of merger.', 'George, because Diane died intestate and her fee simple passed to him as her intestate heir.', 'Sam, because the interest granted to Diane’s spouse is void under the Rule Against Perpetuities.', 'Sam, because Diane, although sur- vived by her spouse, died without children.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('310', 'mbe', 'mbe_0310', 'prompt_0623', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'torts', '84', '', 'Assume, for this question, that Harriet sued Jenny for defamation and that dur- ing the trial Jenny falsely stated on the witness stand that Harriet had com- mingled client funds with her personal assets on other occasions. IfHarriet sues Jenny for defamation for the in-court false statement, Jenny''s best defense will be:', 'Harriet assumed the risk of the in-court statement when she filed the first suit.', 'Jenny''s statement is protected by a conditional privilege.', 'Jenny''s statement is protected by an absolute privilege. Bl1CJ1IS', 'One is never liable for damages for statements made on the wit- ness stand in a civil suit.', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('311', 'mbe', 'mbe_0311', 'prompt_0624', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'torts', '85', '', 'Assume, for this question, that Aaron, a courtroom spectator, heard Jenny''s false statement on the witness stand. When Aaron returned to his office, he repeated Jenny''s statements to Clyde, a co-worker, but Clyde did not believe the statement. If Harriet sues Aaron for defamation, the result will be:', 'Aaron will prevail because he is protected by the reporter''s privi- lege.', 'Harriet will prevail because Aaron has republished the de- famatory statement.', 'Harriet will prevail because Aaron has exceeded the scope of his conditional privilege.', 'Aaron will prevail because Clyde did not believe the statement. Questions 86 - 88 are based on the following facts: Snowco is a manufacturer of snowmobiles. Ski-Fun, an independent retailer, sells a num- ber of snowmobiles from Snowco''s line. Marlene purchased a Snowco snowmobile from Ski-Fun. The very first time she oper- ated the vehicle, the steering mechanism malfunctioned, causing the snowmobile to overturn. The snowmobile struck Harold, a bystander. Marlene and Harold both suffered serious', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('312', 'mbe', 'mbe_0312', 'prompt_0625', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'torts', '86', '', 'If Marlene sues Snowco for injuries suffered in the accident, the likely re- sult will be:', 'Marlene will not recover because she is not in privity of contract with Snowco.', 'Marlene will not recover, unless she can prove Snowco was neg- ligent.', 'Marlene will recover, under a strict liability in tort theory, be- cause Snowco is responsible for the design defect.', 'Marlene will recover, under a strict liability in tort theory, be- cause Snowco is responsible for the manufacturing defect.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('313', 'mbe', 'mbe_0313', 'prompt_0626', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'torts', '87', '', 'Assume, for this question, that Ski-Fun did not inspect the snowmobile''s steer- ing mechanism before it sold the vehicle to Marlene. If Harold sues Ski-Fun for his injuries under a strict liability in tort theory, in most jurisdictions today:', 'Harold will not recover because Ski-Fun did not cause the manu- facturing defect.', 'Harold will recover, so long as he was a reasonably foreseeable bystander.', 'Harold will not recover because he was not in privity with Ski-Fun or Snowco.', 'Harold will recover, but only if he was related to Marlene.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('314', 'mbe', 'mbe_0314', 'prompt_0627', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'torts', '88', '', 'Which of the following statements would constitute Snowco''s best defense to a strict liability in tort action by Marlene?', 'The evidence shows that Marlene tampered with the steer- ing mechanism before she drove the snowmobile.', 'A snowmobile is an inherently dangerous instrument, and Marlene assumed the risk when she operated it.', 'Marlene had never driven a snowmobile before the accident. 100IS faI', 'In the snowmobile industry, Snowco has the most elaborate quality control checks during the manufacturing process. Ell lU''IlS Questions 89 - 90 are based on the following facts: Moribundia is a rare tropical disease that causes death to 98% of humans who con- tract it. There has been no known cure for the disease. Recently, Pharmco developed a new drug to counteract Moribundia. Labo- ratory tests revealed, however, that a side effect of the drug is deafness in 1% of pa- tients who were treated w', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('315', 'mbe', 'mbe_0315', 'prompt_0628', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'torts', '89', '', 'If Elmer sues Pharmco under a strict liability in tort theory for his deafness, the likely result will be:', 'Elmer will recover because Pharmco is responsible for the manufacturing defect.', 'Elmer will recover because Pharmco is responsible for the design defect.', 'Elmer will not recover because the drug was unavoidably un- safe.', 'Elmer will not recover because drug manufacturers are exempt from strict liability rules.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('316', 'mbe', 'mbe_0316', 'prompt_0629', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'torts', '90', '', 'Assume, for this question, that Pharmco''s new drug is available only by prescription and that the package contains a conspicuous warning of the possible deafness side-effect. Assume, also, that Elmer''s doctor administered the drug, but that he failed to tell Elmer of the risk of deafness. If Elmer sues Pharmco under a strict liability in tort theory for his deafness, the result will be:', 'Elmer will recover because Pharmco failed to warn him of the deafness side effect.', 'Elmer will recover because Pharmco is responsible for all personal injuries caused by its drug.', 'Elmer will not recover because Pharmco''s warning to Elmer''s doctor constitutes a warning to Elmer.', 'Elmer will not recover because drug manufacturers are exempt from strict liability rules. Questions 91 - 92 are based on the following facts: Blaster Co. is a manufacturer of dynamite, nitroglycerin and other volatile products. The management at Blaster Co. takes pride in its excellent safety record. It runs a state- of-the-art training program for employees, and constantly demands that employees act with the utmost care. Careless employees are summarily dismissed. Rudy, a Blaster Co. employee,', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('317', 'mbe', 'mbe_0317', 'prompt_0630', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'torts', '91', '', 'If Susan sues Blaster Co. for injuries she suffered when she broke her arm:', 'Blaster Co. should be held strictly liable because it is en- gaged in abnormally dangerous activity.', 'Blaster Co. should be held liable because its employee was negli- gent.', 'Blaster Co. should not be liable because the injury was not rea- sonably foreseeable.', 'Blaster Co. should not be liable because Susan''s injury is cov- ered by worker''s compensation. TCJiIS EJ', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('318', 'mbe', 'mbe_0318', 'prompt_0631', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'torts', '92', '', 'Blaster Co. ''s safety controls and its policy of summarily dismissing care- less employees will:', 'Prevent Susan from recovering from Blaster Co., because Rudy was engaged in a frolic and de- tour.', 'Prevent Susan from recovering from Blaster Co., because Rudy was acting beyond the scope of his employment.', 'Not prevent Susan from recov- ering from Rudy only.', 'Not prevent Susan from recov- ering from both Rudy and Blaster Co. Questions 93 - 94 are based on the following facts: Arlene purchased a toaster which was manu- factured by Crispco. The first time she used the toaster, a wire shorted and caused a fire. Arlene''s kitchen was destroyed and she re- ceived minor burns in attempting to extin- guish the blaze. An investigation revealed that the type of wire in question, which was used in all Crispco toasters, was inadequate to safely conduct normal ho', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('319', 'mbe', 'mbe_0319', 'prompt_0632', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'torts', '93', '', 'IfArlene sues Crispco for injuries to her kitchen and person, the most appropri- ate cause of action would be:', 'A suit for strict liability in tort, because the toaster had a manu- facturing defect.', 'A suit for strict liability in tort, because the toaster had a design defect.', 'A suit for strict liability in tort, because Crispco failed to warn of the danger.', 'A negligence action, provided Arlene can show that Crispco and she were in privity.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('32', 'mbe', 'mbe_0032', 'prompt_0106', 'Barbri - Drills and Released Questions - MDR (MBE - 2007)', 'property', '148', '', 'Assume for the purposes of this question only that Diane died survived by George and two children, Ann and Bradley. Brad- ley dies intestate two days after Diane, leaving one child, Curtis, as his only heir. What are the respective interests of George, Ann, and Curtis in the avocado ranch?', 'The Supremacy Clause.', 'The Army and Navy Clause.', 'The Commerce Clause.', 'The Privileges and Immunities Clause of the Fourteenth Amendment. Questions 150-151 are based on the following fact situation: Cheryl is on trial for fraud, it having been alleged that she participated in an illegal scheme in which her victims were invited to become local distributors for a supposed cosmetics manufacturer. The victims were given bonus payments, after they had made a large initial “investment,” for additional distributors who they would bring to the manufacturer. Cheryl’s defense', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('320', 'mbe', 'mbe_0320', 'prompt_0633', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'torts', '94', '', 'Assume, for this question, that the in- adequate wire caused the toaster to be destroyed, but that Arlene and her kitchen were not harmed. IfArlene sues Crispco in tort to recover the cost of a replacement toaster, the result will be: Q', 'Arlene will recover because the toaster had a manufacturing de- fect.', 'Arlene will recover because the toaster had a design defect.', 'Arlene will recover because Crispco breached its implied warranty of merchantability.', 'Arlene will not recover. Questions 95 — 96 are based on the following facts: Sandra, a physician, was driving to the bal- let with a friend. A car driven by Don had overturned on the road ahead and Don was severely injured. Emergency paramedics had not arrived on the scene, and Sandra could see that Don was bleeding. Although Sandra believed she could stop the bleeding, she decided to continue on her journey to the ballet so that she would not miss the first act. Don died from loss of blood. He', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('321', 'mbe', 'mbe_0321', 'prompt_0634', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'contracts', '95', '', 'If Don''s wife and children sue Sandra for wrongful death:', 'They will recover because a phy- sician has a common law duty to stop and assist an accident vic- tim.', 'They will recover because most states modernly require physi- cians to stop and assist an acci- dent victim.', 'They will recover because the American Medical Association''s Principles of Medical Ethics re- quire physicians to render emer- gency medical services to the best of their ability.', 'They will not recover. TORTS 255', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('322', 'mbe', 'mbe_0322', 'prompt_0635', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'contracts', '96', '', 'Assume, for this question, that Sandra stopped to assist Don, but that her treat- ment was below the professional stan- dard ofcare and Don died. IfDon''s wife and children sue Sandra for wrongful death, in a state that has a Good Samari- tan statute:', 'They will recover because Sandra undertook a duty and per- formed it negligently.', 'They will recover because Good Samaritan statutes provide phy- sician liability for negligent acts.', 'They will recover, assuming they can prove Sandra acted reck- lessly in the circumstances.', 'They will recover, but only to the extent that Sandra was negligent and aggravated Don''s injuries. Em mus Questions 97 - 99 are based on the following facts: Clara learned she was pregnant with her first child. Clara''s family had a history of con- genital birth defects, so she went to her doc- tor, Anna, for tests to determine if her fetus would likely be born in an impaired state. Anna performed amniocentesis, a procedure that can detect chromosomal abnormalities. Anna negligently misread the t', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('323', 'mbe', 'mbe_0323', 'prompt_0636', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'contracts', '97', '', 'IfRandolph sues Anna for damages, in most jurisdictions, his cause of action would be called a suit for:', 'Wrongful birth.', 'Wrongful life.', 'Wrongful death.', 'None of the above.', 'B', '', '', 0, '')
